      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if i use the hoop stress equations to figure out the minimum thickness needed to prevent structural collapse from the internal pressure, it actually lines up with the seismic data—the way the waves bounce off the interior feels like hitting a hard boundary rather than a gradual transition. \n\nif we treat $R_{in}$ as a variable, can you help me run a quick calc? i want to see what the density of the shell has to be to maintain the observed lunar mass if the cavity $(R_{in})$ is roughly 70% of the total radius. i'm basically trying to prove this isn't just a core-mantle discrepancy, but a literal shell structure.",
